er-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 2 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: The task is a classic synthesis problem: taking three structured inputs (the options) and integrating them into a single cohesive artifact (the recommendation report) with specific sections (summaries, constraints, costs, etc.). The strength 'scaffold via a :code-built outline' directly maps to the requirement for specific named sections (:quality-comparison, :fit-to-team-constraints) to ensure full coverage of the team's context.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Synthesis integrates multiple structured inputs into a single cohesive artifact (narrative / report / recommendation). Always provide the synthesizer with inputs structured by source-id (not free-form concat) so attribution stays intact. For long artifacts, scaffold via a :code-built outline that the :llm fills section-by-section — preserves coverage. NEVER synthesize >5 substantive inputs in one call: do hierarchical clustering instead. Declare :output-schemas on the synthesis output when downstream stages (critique / validation) need to verify specific sections. Composes into briefing-generation, comparative-summary, and sequential-pipeline shells. Avoid when the task is ideation (new options